@article{dailysun1952appointees,
  author      = {{The Daily Sun}},
  title       = {Judge Names 3 County Board Appointees: Picks Byrne, Tillema,
                 Massey; Replaces Dugan, Dean And Cox `Til After Nov. 4 Vote},
  journaltitle = {The Daily Sun},
  location    = {Arlington, Virginia},
  date        = {1952-09-11},
  pages       = {1},
  url         = {https://virginiachronicle.com/?a=d&d=TDS19520911.1.1},
  urldate     = {2026-09-25},
  annotation  = {Vol. XVII, no. 65. The three September 1952 appointees, each
                 with a street. Read against the page image on 25 September
                 2026: all three quotes and house numbers are as printed.
                 Prints Tillema's as North Howard Street where the 1950 census
                 sheet reads N. Harvard St. and Arlington has no North Howard
                 Street, so this is the paper's misprint; and Massey's as North
                 23rd Street where the sheet reads 23rd Rd, two real streets
                 both in North Arlington. The same page carries the governance
                 of the ouster, read off the page text Virginia Chronicle
                 serves at f=XML on 1 October 2026, which is a different OCR
                 from the filed copy's. Judge Walter T. McCarthy announced he
                 would order a special election on 4 November 1952 to replace
                 the three members the Supreme Court of Appeals had removed
                 (paolicelli1952), and political leaders "were caught by
                 surprise... since the filing deadline for candidates in the
                 general County Board election November 4 has passed. But Judge
                 McCarthy said that under Virginia law the filing deadline for
                 special election is only 30 days in advance of the election."
                 The terms: "The four-year term of Board Chairman Robert W. Cox
                 expired December 31, 1953: ! Dugan's, December 31, 1954, and i
                 Alan L. Dean's December 31, 1955" - the paper's "expired" for
                 expires, and its stray characters. Commonwealth Attorney
                 William J. Hassan is quoted that "there will be four separate
                 County Board elections November 4", the three unexpired terms
                 and the seat Alfred E. Frisbie was vacating on 31 December,
                 and that candidates "must file separately for the specific
                 unexpired term which they want to fill as a result of the
                 court ruling". Daniel A. Dugan, "who was one of the three
                 members ousted from the board, is not at present a Government
                 worker but was when elected to the board", said he would run
                 for his own unexpired term; Hal Flood Hughes also announced.
                 T. Oscar Smith, moderator of the 3 September Town Meeting,
                 would ask for another session to nominate Non-Partisan
                 candidates; Leonard O. Hilder for the Democrats and Robert L.
                 Groom for the Republicans would fill out their tickets. The
                 page also dates the circuit ruling the appeal came from: the
                 Supreme Court decision "upheld a May 5 ruling of his", where
                 the opinion itself affirms a decree of 8 May and names no
                 judge (paolicelli1952); the conflict is recorded, not
                 resolved. Filed in Drive as "press/Daily Sun/Daily Sun 1952 -
                 Judge Names 3 County Board Appointees (11 September 1952, p.
                 1).pdf"},
}

