@report{rcva2026evaluation,
  author      = {{FairVote, Ranked Choice Voting Resource Center and Ranked
                 Choice Virginia}},
  title       = {Report to Arlington County Board: Ranked Choice Voting
                 Evaluation},
  institution = {FairVote},
  date        = {2026-02-23},
  annotation  = {The evaluation delivered under the County's 2025 grant, and
                 the only source for three of the four RCV claims the
                 community-input outline makes. Not a County document: Ranked
                 Choice Virginia is this report's co-author's own organisation,
                 and the County's own account of the survey
                 (arlingtonboard2026rcv) is a summary of this one. Reports a
                 post-election survey administered by SurveyUSA, fielded 6 to
                 13 November 2025 from a voter-file sample matched to contact
                 details, 584 completes across four contact methods, weighted
                 to the 2025 electorate on age, gender, race and ethnicity,
                 margin of error plus or minus 5 points on the full sample and
                 wider on subgroups. Anticipation: 71 per cent knew in advance
                 they would use RCV, 26 learned at the ballot, 3 were unsure;
                 45 per cent among Latino voters. Marking: 80 per cent very
                 confident, 16 mostly confident, 4 low; 12 per cent of voters
                 aged 18 to 29 not very confident against 3 per cent of those
                 30 and over. Ranking: 56 per cent ranked more than one
                 candidate, 35 exactly one; of the latter 25 per cent feared
                 ranking more could hurt their favourite, which is roughly 9
                 per cent of all voters. Counting: 62 per cent confident and 25
                 pretty sure, 13 not sure or not understanding, rising to 23
                 per cent among Latino and Asian voters and those 65 and over.
                 On the same weighted full sample, 52 per cent correctly named
                 Takis Karantonis the winner and 47 were unsure, which is the
                 other half of the 87 per cent pairing. Support for future use:
                 36 per cent strongly and 30 somewhat against 11 somewhat and
                 15 strongly opposed, net plus 41; net support plus 58 among
                 renters against plus 39 among homeowners, plus 19 among Latino
                 voters, plus 26 among those 65 and over. Compares the three
                 waves on sample composition only: White 89, 72 and 68 per cent
                 and homeowners 83, 72 and 68 per cent across 2023, 2024 and
                 2025, and states that the age bands cannot be compared because
                 the earlier forms reported 60 and over where this one groups
                 65 and over. Notes that the County has not released a cast
                 vote record, which would be the external check on self-
                 reported ranking. Filed in Drive as "reports/FairVote 2026 -
                 Report to Arlington County Board, Ranked Choice Voting
                 Evaluation.pdf"},
}

